{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During covering and button-triggered exchanges, the policy generally selected the correct button waypoint but sometimes replaced a target coordinate using an airborne cover's apparent top center. Conversely, some annotations attached an old coordinate to a newly retained frame without preserving the intervening identity correspondence. During manipulation, annotations also refined coordinates without sufficient visible geometry. These are PREDICTABILITY failures and can become SUFFICIENCY failures when selecting a container. Keep the ordered list of last reliably measured target-axis [x,y] coordinates, using only evidence available through the annotated cycle. Initial covering alone is not evidence of horizontal displacement: preserve the cube coordinate unless reliable geometry establishes translation. During an exchange, update an entry only when both correspondence and actual surface height are resolved. A clear image center alone is insufficient. Resting-height projection requires visible support or settling evidence; an elevated cover requires independently resolved geometry. Do not use later settling or the recorded waypoint to justify an earlier estimate. During grasping, partial occlusion is acceptable if opposing outer edges or corners in the available calibrated views constrain the center; otherwise preserve the estimate. Audit previously annotated refinements and identity changes under this rule, rather than requiring the policy to reproduce unsupported values. Once an entry's pickup is completed, freeze that entry: subsequent lowering and withdrawal use the visible held object and TCP, not its historical coordinate. Example: [[0.132, 0.013], [0.012, 0.130]].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "During exchanges, a single retained localization frame omitted the intervening motion while target_locations remained stale. The next clip could not establish which identical cover belonged to the requested color. During early pickups, some annotated replacement frames also hid a still-needed second target. This breaks SUFFICIENCY and PREDICTABILITY. Change this field to an ordered trajectory-keyframe list. Its first frame is a synchronized anchor in which every still-needed target has unambiguous identity and reliable world localization consistent with the stored coordinates. While no replacement anchor is available, retain that anchor and every subsequently shown front-camera frame, in chronological order with duplicate labels removed. These frames preserve correspondence even when elevation prevents coordinate updates. When a shown frame establishes a new synchronized anchor for all still-needed targets, discard the earlier prefix, retain that frame, and retain every later shown frame through the current cycle. Evaluate still-needed targets after updating pickups_completed. A remembered coordinate does not make an occluded target visible, and a completed target's visibility does not qualify a frame in place of an unseen remaining target. Initialize to [E0]. Example of an unresolved exchange: [E55, E71, E87, E88]. Annotate causally; do not resolve an ambiguous crossing using later images unavailable at that cycle.",
      "value_type": "keyframe"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During covering and exchanges, correct button actions were accompanied by unsupported coordinate updates from moving cover tops; later pickup selection also suffered when old coordinates were treated as current identity assignments. During grasping, small annotated refinements were not always visibly justified. These failures concern PREDICTABILITY and sometimes SUFFICIENCY. Keep the requested-order list of last reliable central-axis [x,y] measurements. A cover descending onto a known cube does not alone justify changing its coordinate. Trace identity through the retained trajectory frames and the current clip before measuring a new position. Separate identity tracking from metric localization: visible motion may establish correspondence without establishing height. Use resting-top projection only when support or settling is established by the available observations; otherwise require resolved elevated geometry or carry the prior coordinate. Never use nearest stale coordinate or spatial rank to force an ambiguous correspondence. At a grasp, use visible opposing outer edges or corners and the calibrated views to reconstruct the full top center despite finger occlusion, but carry the estimate if those constraints are insufficient. Do not infer the center from the desired waypoint or assume every small pixel shift is translation. After incorporating a successful pickup, freeze that completed entry and continue updating remaining entries. Example: [[0.132, 0.013], [0.012, 0.130]]."
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "A single old frame plus a stale coordinate lost the identity trajectory across exchanges, while some replacement frames hid the next requested target. This made both future target selection and the memory update unpredictable. Store an ordered list, initially [E0]. Read its first frame as the synchronized localization anchor and replay all its later frames followed by the current clip chronologically. Append every newly shown front-camera label, removing duplicates. Incorporate successful lifts before deciding which targets remain needed. A replacement anchor qualifies only if all remaining targets have unambiguous correspondence and reliable world localization in that frame, consistent with the updated coordinates; use only the views actually supplied for that label. If one qualifies, keep the latest qualifying frame and every subsequent shown frame, discarding the earlier prefix. Otherwise retain the entire accumulated list. Thus moving or partly occluded frames can be retained as trajectory evidence without qualifying as localization anchors. Neither a clear image center at unresolved height nor a remembered coordinate for an invisible target qualifies. Do not compress across an unresolved crossing or pretend that retaining frames resolves genuinely ambiguous identity. Example: [E55, E71, E87, E88]."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "During button-triggered exchanges, the button waypoints were generally correct, but the old instruction to establish correspondence from one tracking frame and target_locations could leave later container selection underdetermined. This is a SUFFICIENCY failure. Replace that reference with chronological replay of the retained tracking_frame list followed by the current motion clip. Treat target_locations as last reliable measurements, not guaranteed current positions. Before approaching or grasping a requested container, require an identity-resolved, reliably localized current target; do not select the nearest cover to a stale coordinate. If ongoing motion or temporary occlusion prevents localization, use the existing purposeful settling wait or safe revealing withdrawal and reassess. Do not assume that waiting will resolve a genuinely ambiguous completed exchange. Keep the existing button milestones, button localization, grasp heights, lift/release rules, and nonidentical-waypoint safeguards. After a successful lift, use current TCP x,y and orientation for lowering and empty-hand withdrawal, so no update to a completed target's historical coordinate is required.",
  "refused": "Reports demanding particular old single-frame labels are not adopted literally. Their valid concern was the distinction between visible identity and reliable metric localization, but retaining only the last metric frame still discards motion needed to recognize identical covers in subsequent clips. The trajectory-list replacement addresses both concerns. Reports asking for completed-target coordinate updates during lift and release are also not adopted: those coordinates are not needed for later pickup selection, and lowering is specified from current TCP and visible geometry. Unsupported millimetre-scale refinements must not be manufactured from recorded waypoints or hindsight. Claims that partial occlusion necessarily forbids localization are not adopted as blanket rules; visible opposing geometry in the available views can suffice, but a remembered coordinate alone cannot. No episode-specific button offsets, exchange identities, or grasp corrections are added. The reported button waypoints were within the required positional tolerance, and the existing visual button-centering rule already covers them. A report naming no missing information does not justify an action-rule change; keyframe eligibility must instead be reannotated under the causal visibility rule."
}

